  "final_answer": "I have successfully found the latest research paper on black holes published in Nature Astronomy. Here is the answer:\n\n## Latest Black Hole Research Paper in Nature Astronomy\n\n**Title:** \"A universal critical accretion rate for black hole jet formation\"\n\n**Authors:** Adelle J. Goodwin & Andrew Mummery\n\n**Journal:** Nature Astronomy (2026)\n\n**Published:** 17 September 2026\n\n**DOI/URL:** https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02951-1\n\n**Abstract (summary):** The paper demonstrates that tidal disruption events (TDEs) launch outflows during a super-Eddington accretion phase and a second, physically distinct outflow at a critical accretion rate of Lcrit ≈ 0.02 LEdd — the same as the critical accretion rate for stellar-mass black holes. This finding supports the long-suspected idea that black hole accretion–outflow coupling is **invariant from stellar to supermassive scales**. Using TDEs as a unique observational window (since supermassive black hole accretion evolves over years rather than thousands of years in AGN), the authors identified a universal critical accretion threshold at which jets are launched.\n\n**Engagement:** 360 accesses and 326 Altmetric score at the time of retrieval.\n\nThis was identified by directly browsing Nature Astronomy's 2026 article listing and filtering for the most recent black hole–related publication.",
